===========================
HIGH COHESION – INTERVIEW NOTES
===========================

Definition
----------
High Cohesion means a class or module should have one well-defined responsibility,
and all its methods and data should work together to achieve that single purpose.

Interview Definition (30 sec)
-----------------------------
"High cohesion is a design principle where a class focuses on one responsibility,
with all its methods and data closely related. Highly cohesive classes are easier
to understand, test, maintain, reuse, and extend because changes remain localized."

Why High Cohesion?
------------------
• Easier to understand
• Easier to maintain
• Easier to test
• Better code reuse
• Fewer bugs
• Changes are localized
• Improves readability
• Easier debugging
• Easier extension

Example
-------

❌ Low Cohesion

class Employee
{
public:
    void calculateSalary();
    void saveToDatabase();
    void sendEmail();
    void printReport();
};

Problems:
• Multiple unrelated responsibilities
• Difficult to maintain
• Hard to test
• Multiple reasons to change


✅ High Cohesion

class SalaryCalculator
{
public:
    double calculateSalary();
};

class EmployeeRepository
{
public:
    void save();
};

class EmailService
{
public:
    void sendEmail();
};

class ReportPrinter
{
public:
    void print();
};

Each class has one clear responsibility.

--------------------------------------------------

High Cohesion vs Low Cohesion
-----------------------------

High Cohesion                    Low Cohesion
------------------------------------------------------------
One responsibility               Multiple responsibilities
Focused class                    God/Bloated class
Easy to maintain                 Hard to maintain
Easy to test                     Hard to test
Reusable                         Poor reusability
Localized changes                Changes affect unrelated code

--------------------------------------------------

High Cohesion vs SRP
--------------------

High Cohesion                    Single Responsibility Principle
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Design principle                 SOLID principle
Related functionality together   One reason to change
Focus on class internals         Focus on responsibility boundary

Relationship:
Following SRP usually results in High Cohesion.

--------------------------------------------------

High Cohesion vs Low Coupling
-----------------------------

High Cohesion                    Low Coupling
------------------------------------------------------------
Inside a class                   Between classes
Related functionality            Minimal dependencies
Single responsibility            Loose interaction

Good Design = High Cohesion + Low Coupling

--------------------------------------------------

Real-world Example
------------------

Car Components

Engine      -> Generates power
Brakes      -> Stops the car
Steering    -> Changes direction

Each component performs one specific job.
This is High Cohesion.

